            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "[Recent conversation context:]\n- cameron_pfiffer: **Letta v0.14.0 is now out!**\n\nWe highly recommend that self-hosted users upgrade to this version and begin migrating to the v1 SDK.\n\n- Letta v1 SDK support\n- Archival memory improvements\n- Webhooks for step completions\n- Parallel tool calling \n- New message types\n\nRead the forum post for more details: https://forum.letta.com/t/announcement-letta-v0-14-0/124\n- cameron_pfiffer: Hey y'all, we're releasing an early preview of the [Agentic Learning SDK](https://github.com/letta-ai/agentic-learning-sdk)!\n\n**The Learning SDK makes everything stateful!**\n\nThe Learning SDK is designed to provide a convenient wrappers to add memory to pretty much anything that calls a language model, using Python or TypeScript. This includes Anthropic, the Claude Agent SDK, OpenAI completions, OpenAI responses, Gemini, Vercel AI SDK.\n\nThe Learning SDK is essentially a thin wrapper around a Letta agent, and will intercept, inject, and remember anything that goes through an inference call. \n\nThe general pattern is to wrap an inference call with `learning`. In this example, we're going to add a Letta agent's state to a simple OpenAI completions request, which many codebases currently have:\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom agentic_learning import learning\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Add continual learning with one line\nwith learning(agent=\"my_assistant\"):\n    # All LLM calls inside this block have learning enabled\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-5\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"My name is Alice\"}]\n    )\n\n    # wait a little bit for the Letta agent to remember stuff\n\n    # Agent remembers prior context\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-5\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What's my name?\"}]\n    )\n    # Returns: \"Your name is Alice\"\n```\nAlternatively, you can use Typescript with a similar pattern:\n```ts\nimport { learning } from '@letta-ai/agentic-learning';\nimport OpenAI from 'openai';\n\nconst client = new OpenAI();\n\n// Add continual learning with one line\nawait learning({ agent: \"my_assistant\" }, async () => {\n    // All LLM calls inside this block have learning enabled\n    const response = await client.chat.completions.create({\n        model: \"gpt-5\",\n        messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"My name is Alice\" }]\n    });\n\n    // wait a little bit for the Letta agent to remember stuff\n\n    // Agent remembers prior context\n    const response2 = await client.chat.completions.create({\n        model: \"gpt-5\",\n        messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"What's my name?\" }]\n    });\n    // Returns: \"Your name is Alice\"\n});\n```\n\n## Features\n\n- Supports the vast majority of tools you might want state in -- use whatever your current stack is\n- Simple learning context scoped to agent name, such as `support_agent`, `sales_bot`, `personal_information`, etc.\n- Make anything stateful\n- Automatic context injection, which you can disable if you only want logging/remembering without state being included\n- Semantic search across agent knowledge with `learning_client.memory.search(agent=\"personal_info\", query=\"how sad am I\")`\n\n## How it works\n\nIt's pretty straightforward. Basically, the learning SDK will:\n\n- Intercept messages before and after they go to the inference provider\n- Inject Letta's memory context into the prompt (unless disabled)\n- Learns the retrieved prompt by sending it to the Letta agent\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall on Python with the following:\n```\npip install agentic-learning --prerelease=allow\n```\n`prelease` is only required while the Letta v1 SDK is in the pre-release phase, you won't need this in the future. \n\nFor Typescript, install with:\n```\nnpm install @letta-ai/agentic-learning\n```\n\nThanks very much to <@1318282422961573959>  on our team for putting the Learning SDK together. She did an excellent job.\n\nLearn more about the SDK here: https://github.com/letta-ai/agentic-learning-sdk\nFull announcement on the forum: https://forum.letta.com/t/announcing-the-agentic-learning-sdk-add-state-to-anything/125\n\n-- Cameron\n- cameron_pfiffer: Our documentation page has been updated: https://docs.letta.com. Please notify me if you see anything off!\n- cameron_pfiffer: **Request for interest**\nIf you have wanted to build an application to allows users to sign in to their Letta account to use their own accounts, we have some early alpha tooling to support OAuth for your application. This would give you a \"Sign in with Letta\" button that you can use to manage user accounts. \n\nWe think this is probably a good move for people who want to resell or build on top of the Letta developer platform.\n\n**What you could build with \"Sign in with Letta\":**\n\n• **Custom chat interfaces** - Voice agents, mobile apps, specialized UIs connected to user's Letta agents\n• **Agent marketplaces** - \"Deploy to Letta\" buttons for sharing and installing community agent templates\n• **IDE integrations** - VS Code extensions, coding assistants that access user's agents\n• **Voice platforms** - Vapi/LiveKit integrations without managing separate auth\n• **Multi-agent networks** - Platforms that coordinate agents across user accounts\n• **Analytics dashboards** - Third-party tools for monitoring agent performance, costs, memory usage\n• **Browser extensions** - ChatGPT memory layers, productivity tools (already being built by community)\n• **Integration platforms** - Richer Zapier/n8n workflows, CRM connections to customer service agents\n• **Memory tools** - Backup/export services, cross-agent search, knowledge base builders\n\n**Why it matters:** Build Letta-powered features without managing auth infrastructure. Users connect once, your app accesses their agents securely.\n\n**Interested in alpha access?** Ping me.\n- swooders.: 🌟 **Gemini 3 Pro is now available on the Letta API**! 🌟 \nhttps://x.com/Letta_AI/status/1990898600352895318\n(support will also be in the next release for self-hosted)\n\nYou can select \"Gemini 3 Pro\" from the ADE, or configure it from the SDK: \n```python\nfrom letta_client import Letta\n\n# 1.0 pre-release client \nclient = Letta(api_key=\"LETTA_API_KEY\")\n\nagent = client.agents.create(\n    name=\"gemini\", \n    model=\"google_ai/gemini-3-pro\",\n    model_settings={\n        \"provider_type\": \"google_ai\",\n        \"thinking_config\": {\n            \"include_thoughts\": True\n        }\n    }\n)\n```\n- swooders.: @everyone \n\n## 1️⃣ 1.0 SDK Release \nWe just released the 1.0 SDKs for Python and TS! Overview: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IkpOwzWYi5U\n\nNPM: https://www.npmjs.com/package/@letta-ai/letta-client\nPypi: https://pypi.org/project/letta-client/\n\n⚠️**This is a breaking change, so make sure to pin your version until you migrate existing code. See our [migration guide](https://docs.letta.com/api-reference/sdk-migration-guide) for details on the changes.**⚠️\n\n## Statful Agent Meetup: CLI/Coding Agents \nWe're also hosting a meetup **tomorrow (11/20) in SF** which will cover building coding/CLI agents with the v1 SDK. To register: https://luma.com/stateful-agents\n[End context]\n\n[cameron_pfiffer (id=657626741854044220) sent a message in #📌｜announcements] Office hours in **ten minutes**\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]",
              "signature": null
            }
