        {
          "type": "message",
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "reasoning",
              "is_native": true,
              "reasoning": "Cameron figured out the issue - it's a Zed (the code editor) issue with passing environment variables to the integrated terminal. This is a common IDE problem where the integrated terminal doesn't inherit environment variables the same way a standalone terminal does.\n\nThis is just him acknowledging the root cause. I should probably acknowledge this briefly and maybe offer a quick workaround if helpful, but he's already figured it out so I don't need to be verbose.",
              "signature": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "That would explain it. Zed's integrated terminal environment can be isolated from your shell's env vars.\n\nQuick workaround: Either run from a standalone terminal or add a `.env` file to the project root with `python-dotenv`:\n\n```python\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nload_dotenv()  # Loads from .env file in project root\n```\n\nThat way it'll work regardless of terminal environment inheritance issues.",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
          "name": null,
          "otid": null,
          "sender_id": null,
          "batch_item_id": null,
          "group_id": null,
