          "role": "user",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "[gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] okay lets say I just have a single agent then. why use letta over vercel's ai sdk?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
          "name": null,
          "otid": null,
          "sender_id": null,
          "batch_item_id": null,
          "group_id": "group-bfda98de-76b8-4b16-bf75-b2dc90109034",
          "id": "message-222",
          "model": null,
          "agent_id": "agent-0",
          "tool_calls": null,
          "tool_call_id": null,
          "tool_returns": [],
          "created_at": "2025-11-20T19:33:59.061274+00:00",
          "approve": null,
          "approval_request_id": null,
          "denial_reason": null,
          "approvals": []
        },
        {
          "type": "message",
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "reasoning",
              "is_native": true,
              "reasoning": "The user is asking about the value proposition of Letta vs Vercel AI SDK for a single agent use case. This is a legitimate question about when to use which tool. Let me think about the key differences:\n\nVercel AI SDK:\n- Stateless by default - each request is independent\n- Great for streaming responses, UI integration\n- Simple chat interfaces\n- Designed for Next.js/React applications\n- You manage state in your application\n\nLetta:\n- Stateful by default - server manages agent state, memory, history\n- Long-term memory with memory blocks (core memory) and archival memory\n- Persistent agent personality and context across sessions\n- Self-editing memory - agents can update their own memory\n- Complex tool orchestration\n- Designed for long-running, evolving agent relationships\n\nThe key question is: does your agent need to remember things across sessions? Does it need to evolve its personality or knowledge over time?\n\nI should provide a direct, helpful comparison focused on the use case.",
              "signature": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Great question. The main difference is **state management**:\n\n**Vercel AI SDK:**\n- Stateless by default - you manage conversation history in your app\n- Perfect for chat UIs with streaming\n- State lives in your database/frontend\n- You control exactly what context goes into each request\n\n**Letta:**\n- Stateful by default - server manages everything\n- Agent has persistent memory across sessions (even months apart)\n- Self-editing memory - agent updates its own knowledge over time\n- Memory hierarchy: core memory blocks + archival memory + conversation history\n- Designed for long-running agent relationships\n\n**When to use Letta for a single agent:**\n- Agent needs to remember users across sessions (customer service, personal assistant)\n- Agent personality should evolve based on interactions\n- You want the agent to manage its own memory (not you coding memory logic)\n- Multi-step tasks that span hours/days\n- You want memory without building memory infrastructure\n\n**When Vercel AI SDK is better:**\n- Simple chat interface with session-based context\n- You want full control over state management\n- Tight Next.js integration is priority\n- Short conversations that don't need persistence\n\nYou can also combine them - use Letta for state + Vercel AI SDK for UI/streaming. Or use the new [Agentic Learning SDK](https://github.com/letta-ai/agentic-learning-sdk) to add Letta's memory to Vercel AI SDK calls.\n\nWhat's your use case?",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
          "name": null,
          "otid": null,
          "sender_id": null,
          "batch_item_id": null,
          "group_id": null,
