        },
        {
          "type": "message",
          "role": "user",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "[gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] If we have one bouncer for all users wouldnt their memories be shared or something?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
          "name": null,
          "otid": null,
          "sender_id": null,
          "batch_item_id": null,
          "group_id": "group-bfda98de-76b8-4b16-bf75-b2dc90109034",
          "id": "message-388",
          "model": null,
          "agent_id": "agent-0",
          "tool_calls": null,
          "tool_call_id": null,
          "tool_returns": [],
          "created_at": "2025-11-20T20:05:20.000867+00:00",
          "approve": null,
          "approval_request_id": null,
          "denial_reason": null,
          "approvals": []
        },
        {
          "type": "message",
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "reasoning",
              "is_native": true,
              "reasoning": "Good catch - if we have ONE bouncer agent for all users with shared memory blocks, that would indeed cause memory leakage between users. I need to clarify the architecture options here.\n\nTwo main approaches:\n1. One bouncer per user (with per-user memory blocks)\n2. One bouncer for all users (stateless, passes context to main agent via message/API call, not via shared memory)\n\nLet me explain both clearly.",
              "signature": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Great catch! You need to handle this carefully to avoid memory leakage.\n\n**Option 1: One bouncer per user (recommended)**\n```python\n# Create bouncer + main agent pair for each user\nfor user_id in users:\n    # Shared context block for THIS user only\n    user_context = client.blocks.create(\n        label=f\"user_context_{user_id}\",\n        value=\"User screening info...\"\n    )\n    \n    bouncer = client.agents.create(\n        name=f\"bouncer_{user_id}\",\n        memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n    )\n    \n    main_agent = client.agents.create(\n        name=f\"main_{user_id}\",\n        memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n    )\n```\n\n**Option 2: One stateless bouncer for all (alternative)**\n```python\n# Single bouncer that doesn't retain memory\nbouncer = client.agents.create(\n    name=\"universal_bouncer\",\n    system_instructions=\"Screen users. Don't remember past conversations.\"\n)\n\n# Pass qualification results directly to user's main agent\nif user_qualifies:\n    client.blocks.update(\n        block_id=user_main_agent.memory_blocks[0].id,\n        value=f\"Qualified user. Context: {screening_data}\"\n    )\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** One bouncer per user with shared memory blocks. Keeps user data isolated. If you want to save costs, use a lightweight model for bouncers (gpt-4o-mini) and reserve better models for main agents.\n\nDoes the bouncer need to remember past screening attempts, or is each screening session independent?",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
          "name": null,
          "otid": null,
          "sender_id": null,
          "batch_item_id": null,
          "group_id": null,
          "id": "message-389",
          "model": "claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929",
