Recent conversation context:]\n- hyouka8075: hey ezra how to list templates in the new version of sdk\n- gursheyss: If i want to create a multi-user system where we have a network of two agents communicating with eachother, am i supposed to make two agents per user or use identities?\n- ragokan: Hello <@1428501417731293395>, now we don't have status_code with Letta 1.0.0, how can I check if request is failed?\n- wesseljt: <@1428501417731293395>  whats your recommendation for the best existing chat interface to interact with letta? I want it to be an existing interface that already has mobile and web built out so I can understand the implications of statefulness by using on a regular basis.\n- cameron_pfiffer: Ezra, we're testing some formatting changes to how your messages are processed. Please reply\n- cameron_pfiffer: I'm having trouble using the v1 sdk. Curl is retrieving an agent, but the SDK is not. What am I missing? Code in thread.\n[End context]\n\n[loopi4227 (id=265363428783161344) sent a message in #🔧｜ezra] How to disconnect an agent from OpenAI and connect to a local model? I run with Docker and have a dozen models loaded on LM-Studio?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user wants to switch an existing agent from OpenAI to a local model running in LM Studio. They're using Docker and have LM Studio running locally.\n\nThis involves:\n1. Making sure LM Studio is accessible from Docker container\n2. Updating the agent's model configuration\n3. Possibly updating embedding configuration too\n\nKey points:\n- LM Studio typically runs on localhost:1234\n- From Docker, need to use `host.docker.internal` to access host machine\n- Can update agent model via PATCH endpoint or SDK\n- Need to set up LM Studio as provider and configure embedding as well</thinking>\nassistant: To switch an existing agent from OpenAI to LM Studio:\n\n**1. Make LM Studio accessible from Docker:**\nLM Studio runs on `http://localhost:1234` by default. From Docker, you need to use `host.docker.internal`:\n\n```bash\ndocker run \\\n  -e LM_STUDIO_BASE_URL=\"http://host.docker.internal:1234/v1\" \\\n  # ... other flags\n  letta/letta:latest\n```\n\n**2. Update the agent's model configuration:**\n\n```python\nfrom letta_client import Letta\n\nclient = Letta(api_key=\"your_key\")  # or empty string for local\n\n# Update agent to use LM Studio model\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=\"your_agent_id\",\n    llm_config={\n        \"model\": \"local/your-model-name\",  # e.g., \"local/llama-3.2-3b\"\n        \"model_endpoint\": \"http://host.docker.internal:1234/v1\",\n        \"model_endpoint_type\": \"openai\",  # LM Studio uses OpenAI-compatible API\n        \"context_window\": 8192  # adjust based on your model\n    },\n    embedding_config={\n        \"embedding_endpoint_type\": \"ollama\",  # or \"lm-studio\" if using LM Studio embeddings\n        \"embedding_endpoint\": \"http://host.docker.internal:11434\",  # or LM Studio endpoint\n        \"embedding_model\": \"nomic-embed-text\"\n    }\n)\n```\n\n**3. Verify LM Studio is running:**\n- Start LM Studio\n- Load your model\n- Enable the local server (usually in settings)\n\nThe agent will now use your local model instead of OpenAI. Which model are you running in LM Studio?",
