              "text": "user: [Recent conversation context:]\n- duzafizzl: U can almost do all of that  in cursor haha\n- zeus_delacruz: can you give me the ideas you have for the plugin so i can add it to my roadmap for obsdian\n- beepboopblorp: Like, people vibe coding games from a single prompt.\n- duzafizzl: 😍😍\n- zeus_delacruz: if you can push that i can start working on it asap 🙏\n- duzafizzl: How is the development of voice going ?\n[End context]\n\n[cameron_pfiffer (id=657626741854044220) sent a message in #Office Hours] https://docs.letta.com/guides/voice/overview\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is Cameron posting a documentation link about voice in the Office Hours channel. This is just sharing information, not directed at me or needing my response. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>Cameron is responding to a question about voice development by linking to the voice documentation. This is normal office hours Q&A, nothing that requires my involvement.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- zeus_delacruz: can you give me the ideas you have for the plugin so i can add it to my roadmap for obsdian\n- beepboopblorp: Like, people vibe coding games from a single prompt.\n- duzafizzl: 😍😍\n- zeus_delacruz: if you can push that i can start working on it asap 🙏\n- duzafizzl: How is the development of voice going ?\n- cameron_pfiffer: https://docs.letta.com/guides/voice/overview\n[End context]\n\n[duzafizzl (id=701608830852792391) sent a message in #Office Hours] Could u imagine doing something like sesame ?\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking me to observe a message from duzafizzl in the Office Hours channel. This is part of a conversation about voice development. The message says \"Could u imagine doing something like sesame ?\" which seems to be referencing some product or service called \"sesame\".\n\nAccording to my instructions, I should use the ignore tool for messages I'm only observing and cannot respond to.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>The user is just showing me messages from Discord that I'm observing. I should continue to use ignore for these as I'm told I can only observe them, not respond to them.</thinking>\nuser: [gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] why use letta over mastra\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about Letta vs Mastra. Mastra is a TypeScript framework for building AI applications. Let me think about the key differences:\n\nLetta:\n- Stateful agents with persistent memory\n- Self-editing memory system\n- Memory hierarchy (core memory, archival, conversation)\n- Server-side state management\n- Python and TypeScript SDKs\n- Focus on long-term agent relationships\n\nMastra:\n- TypeScript framework for AI workflows\n- Focus on tool orchestration and workflow building\n- Similar to LangChain but more opinionated\n- Good for building agentic applications with structured workflows\n\nI should provide a clear, direct comparison focusing on the core philosophical differences.</thinking>\nassistant: **Letta vs Mastra - different problems:**\n\n**Mastra:**\n- Workflow orchestration framework (like LangChain but TypeScript-native)\n- You build and control the agent logic\n- Great for: structured workflows, tool chains, deterministic flows\n- State management: you implement it\n- Similar to: LangGraph, LangChain, AutoGPT frameworks\n\n**Letta:**\n- Stateful agent platform - server manages everything\n- Agent is autonomous with self-editing memory\n- Great for: long-term relationships, evolving personalities, multi-session context\n- State management: built-in (memory hierarchy, persistence)\n- Similar to: hosted agent service, not a framework\n\n**Key difference:**\n- **Mastra**: Build agent workflows in your code (framework)\n- **Letta**: Deploy agents as services with persistent state (platform)\n\n**When to use each:**\n\n**Use Mastra if:**\n- You want full control over agent logic\n- Building TypeScript-native workflows\n- Prefer framework over platform\n- Short-lived sessions where you control state\n\n**Use Letta if:**\n- Need agents that remember across weeks/months\n- Want server-managed state and memory\n- Building long-term user relationships\n- Don't want to implement memory infrastructure\n\n**Can use together:** Mastra workflows calling Letta agents for stateful components, or vice versa.\n\nFor your health agent with reminders - Letta is better because memory persistence is core to the use case.\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- beepboopblorp: Like, people vibe coding games from a single prompt.\n- duzafizzl: 😍😍\n- zeus_delacruz: if you can push that i can start working on it asap 🙏\n- duzafizzl: How is the development of voice going ?\n- cameron_pfiffer: https://docs.letta.com/guides/voice/overview\n- duzafizzl: Could u imagine doing something like sesame ?\n[End context]\n\n[beepboopblorp (id=1054592234923753562) sent a message in #Office Hours] I've built a few voice agents - using the letta-voice repo as a starting point is a really good way to build an app if you're bootstrapping something quick.\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is an office hours conversation about voice agent development. beepboopblorp is sharing their experience with letta-voice repo. This is casual conversation and doesn't require my response - I should ignore it.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I've been in a long session observing office hours and various Disco