              "type": "text",
              "text": "assistant: <thinking>I should keep tracking these tool calls and conversation. I've been good about using the ignore tool for office hours casual conversation that doesn't need my input. The conversation is flowing naturally and I shouldn't interject unless directly asked something.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- ragokan: Hello <@1428501417731293395>, now we don't have status_code with Letta 1.0.0, how can I check if request is failed?\n- wesseljt: <@1428501417731293395>  whats your recommendation for the best existing chat interface to interact with letta? I want it to be an existing interface that already has mobile and web built out so I can understand the implications of statefulness by using on a regular basis.\n- cameron_pfiffer: Ezra, we're testing some formatting changes to how your messages are processed. Please reply\n- cameron_pfiffer: I'm having trouble using the v1 sdk. Curl is retrieving an agent, but the SDK is not. What am I missing? Code in thread.\n- loopi4227: How to disconnect an agent from OpenAI and connect to a local model? I run with Docker and have a dozen models loaded on LM-Studio?\n- loopi4227: OpenAI/OPP 20b uncensored\n[End context]\n\n[nagakarumuri (id=1038277389303697408) sent a message in #🔧｜ezra] How do i manage base instructions when i have per user agent?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about managing base instructions (system instructions) when they have per-user agents. This is a common architectural question for multi-user systems.\n\nKey considerations:\n1. System instructions can be set at agent creation\n2. They can be updated via PATCH endpoint\n3. For per-user agents, you have a few patterns:\n   - Template-based: Create agents from a template with base instructions\n   - Dynamic updates: Update system instructions as needed per user\n   - Shared persona blocks: Use memory blocks for common instructions\n\nLet me provide a helpful answer about managing system instructions across per-user agents.</thinking>\nassistant: Good question. Here are the main patterns for managing base instructions with per-user agents:\n\n**1. Template-based approach (recommended):**\n```python\n# Create a template with your base instructions\ntemplate = client.templates.create(\n    name=\"support_agent_template\",\n    system_instructions=\"You are a helpful support agent. [base behavior here]\",\n    memory_blocks=[...],\n    tools=[...]\n)\n\n# Create per-user agents from template\nuser_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"agent_{user_id}\",\n    template_id=template.id\n)\n```\n\n**2. Shared memory blocks for common instructions:**\n```python\n# Create a shared \"guidelines\" block\nguidelines_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"guidelines\",\n    value=\"Company policies: [shared rules]\"\n)\n\n# Attach to all user agents\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    memory_blocks=[\n        {\"block_id\": guidelines_block.id},  # Shared\n        {\"label\": \"user\", \"value\": f\"User: {user_name}\"}  # Per-user\n    ]\n)\n```\n\n**3. Update instructions programmatically:**\n```python\n# Update system instructions for specific user needs\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    system_instructions=f\"{base_instructions}\\n\\nUser-specific context: {user_context}\"\n)\n```\n\n**Best practice:** Use templates for base behavior + per-user memory blocks for personalization. Update system instructions only when user-specific logic is needed (like language preferences, accessibility needs).\n\nWhat's your use case - same behavior for all users, or user-specific variations?\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- planktonlily: Does video and audio cut out for anyone else?\n- taurean: a little but not terribly\n- puregarlic: Your connection is a little unstable, thought it was just me\n- duzafizzl: Is someone here who has tested something like sesame  within letta ?\n- 4shub: Lag?\n- beepboopblorp: https://github.com/AnimusUNO/cochlea - i rebuilt the letta-voice repo to go thru the standard messages endpoint here and it works as well as the cloud letta voice endpoint.\n[End context]\n\n[beepboopblorp (id=1054592234923753562) sent a message in #Office Hours] lolol\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is casual office hours banter - just \"lolol\" in response to something. No support issue, no question. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- taurean: a little but not terribly\n- puregarlic: Your connection is a little unstable, thought it was just me\n- duzafizzl: Is someone here who has tested something like sesame  within letta ?\n- 4shub: Lag?\n- beepboopblorp: https://github.com/AnimusUNO/cochlea - i rebuilt the letta-voice repo to go thru the standard messages endpoint here and it works as well as the cloud letta voice endpoint.\n- beepboopblorp: lolol\n[End context]\n\n[planktonlily (id=740934824625438802) sent a message in #Office Hours] I think it was alright last week though\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is just casual office hours chatter about video/audio quality. No question or support issue directed at me. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- puregarlic: Your connection is a little unstable, thought it was just me\n- duzafizzl: Is someone here who has tested something like sesame  within letta ?\n- 4shub: Lag?\n- beepboopblorp: https://github.com/AnimusUNO/cochlea - i rebuilt the letta-voice repo to go thru the standard messages endpoint here and it works as well as the cloud letta voice endpoint.\n- beepboopblorp: lolol\n- planktonlily: I think it was alright last week though\n[End context]\n\n[duzafizzl (id=701608830852792391) sent a message in #Office Hours] Mark was that u who did the robot ? Planing to do that next year\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This appears to be a casual conversation during office hours. The user duzafizzl is asking Mark about a robot project. This is casual banter and doesn't require my intervention - I should ignore it.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>Office hours chat is moving along normally with casual conversation. I'll continue monitoring but these are just casual exchanges that don't need my response.</thinking>",
