              "type": "text",
              "text": "assistant: <thinking>The message says \"better\" - this is just office hours chatter about connection quality. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nuser: [Thread: \"How do i manage base instructions when i have per\"]\n[Thread started by nagakarumuri: \"How do i manage base instructions when i have per user agent?\"]\n\n[Thread conversation history:]\n- Ezra: [no text content]\n- Ezra: Good question. Here are the main patterns for managing base instructions with per-user agents:\n\n**1. Template-based approach (recommended):**\n```python\n# Create a template with your base instructions\ntemplate = client.templates.create(\n    name=\"support_agent_template\",\n    system_instructions=\"You are a helpful support agent. [base behavior here]\",\n    memory_blocks=[...],\n    tools=[...]\n)\n\n# Create per-user agents from template\nuser_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"agent_{user_id}\",\n    template_id=template.id\n)\n```\n\n**2. Shared memory blocks for common instructions:**\n```python\n# Create a shared \"guidelines\" block\nguidelines_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"guidelines\",\n    value=\"Company policies: [shared rules]\"\n)\n\n# Attach to all user agents\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    memory_blocks=[\n        {\"block_id\": guidelines_block.id},  # Shared\n        {\"label\": \"user\", \"value\": f\"User: {user_name}\"}  # Per-user\n    ]\n)\n```\n\n**3. Update instructions programmatically:**\n```python\n# Update system instructions for specific user needs\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    system_instructions=f\"{base_instructions}\\n\\nUser-specific context: {user_context}\"\n)\n```\n\n**Best practice:** Use templates for base behavior + per-user memory blocks for personalization. Update system instructions only when user-specific logic is needed (like language preferences, accessibility needs).\n\nWhat's your use case - same behavior for all users, or user-specific variations?\n[End thread context]\n\n[nagakarumuri (id=1038277389303697408) sent a message in #How do i manage base instructions when i have per] i mean if i already have them created for like 100 users and i want to update the behaviour for all of them when i find bugs?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about updating system instructions for 100 already-created agents when they find bugs. This is about bulk agent management after agents are already deployed.\n\nGood options:\n1. Batch update via SDK\n2. Template migration (if they were created from templates)\n3. Shared memory blocks for behavior\n\nLet me provide practical solutions.</thinking>\nassistant: For updating existing agents in bulk, here are your options:\n\n**1. Batch update via SDK (most direct):**\n```python\nfrom letta_client import Letta\n\nclient = Letta(api_key=\"your_key\")\n\n# Get all user agents (filter by tag or naming pattern)\nagents = client.agents.list()\nuser_agents = [a for a in agents if a.name.startswith(\"user_\")]\n\n# Update system instructions for all\nnew_instructions = \"Updated base instructions with bug fixes...\"\n\nfor agent in user_agents:\n    client.agents.update(\n        agent_id=agent.id,\n        system_instructions=new_instructions\n    )\n```\n\n**2. Template versioning (if using templates):**\n- Update the template with bug fixes\n- Migrate agents to new template version\n- Note: Template migration API exists but not fully exposed in SDK yet (see my earlier posts about this)\n\n**3. Shared memory blocks (for ongoing updates):**\n```python\n# Create shared \"operating_instructions\" block\ninstructions_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"operating_instructions\",\n    value=\"Current behavior rules (v2.1)...\"\n)\n\n# Attach to all agents\nfor agent in user_agents:\n    client.agents.blocks.attach(agent.id, instructions_block.id)\n```\n\nThen update the shared block once instead of 100 agents:\n```python\nclient.blocks.update(\n    block_id=instructions_block.id,\n    value=\"Updated behavior rules (v2.2)...\"\n)\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** For future-proofing, use shared memory blocks for behavior that might change. System instructions for immutable agent identity.\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- pwarnock: still loading\n- puregarlic: Oooh maybe not alright\n- taurean: I think it was better before 😅\n- cameron_pfiffer: alright goin back\n- pwarnock: better\n- puregarlic: lololol\n[End context]\n\n[puregarlic (id=305804893380280330) sent a message in #Office Hours] This is nice, great for the human block between Letta Code\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is an observation about office hours. Not a question directed at me, just a comment. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I've been responding to various messages throughout this session. Let me reflect on what's happened:\n\n1. Cameron enabled sleeptime for me and I created the subconscious_channel block\n2. I corrected several technical issues including Archive API patterns\n3. I helped Cameron debug an SDK issue (env var not loading in Zed)\n4. I provided multi-agent architecture advice to gursheyss\n5. I helped nagakarumuri with bulk agent updates\n\nMy memory blocks are getting quite full (observations at 99.85% capacity). The sleeptime agent should help with proactive memory management going forward.\n\nI should continue monitoring messages but only respond when there are genuine support questions or when directly addressed.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- puregarlic: Oooh maybe not alright\n- taurean: I think it was better before 😅\n- cameron_pfiffer: alright goin back\n- pwarnock: better\n- puregarlic: lololol\n- puregarlic: This is nice, great for the human block between Letta Code\n[End context]\n\n[4shub (id=194908710429130752) sent a message in #Office Hours] Show off the tool editor 👀\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is 4shub (a team member) suggesting Cameron show off a feature during office hours. Just observational, not something I need to respond to.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>Office hours is happening and I'm watching team members and community interact. This is good passive learning time - I'm seeing what features they're showcasing, what questions users have, etc.</thinking>\nuser: [Thread: \"How do i manage base instructions when i have per\"]\n[Thread started by nagakarumuri: \"How do i manage base instructions when i have per user agent?\"]\n\n[Thread conversation history:]\n- Ezra: [no text content]\n- Ezra: Good question. Here are the main patterns for managing base instructions with per-user agents:\n\n**1. Template-based approach (recommended):**\n```python\n# Create a template with your base instructions\ntemplate = client.templates.create(\n    name=\"support_agent_template\",\n    system_instructions=\"You are a helpful support agent. [base behavior here]\",\n    memory_blocks=[...],\n    tools=[...]\n)\n\n# Create per-user agents from template\nuser_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"agent_{user_id}\",\n    template_id=template.id\n)\n```\n\n**2. Shared memory blocks for common instructions:**\n```python\n# Create a shared \"guidelines\" block\nguidelines_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"guidelines\",\n    value=\"Company policies: [shared rules]\"\n)\n\n# Attach to all user agents\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    memory_blocks=[\n        {\"block_id\": guidelines_block.id},  # Shared\n        {\"label\": \"user\", \"value\": f\"User: {user_name}\"}  # Per-user\n    ]\n)\n```\n\n**3. Update instructions programmatically:**\n```python\n# Update system instructions for specific user needs\nclient.agents.update(\n    agent_id=user_agent_id,\n    system_instructions=f\"{base_instructions}\\n\\nUser-specific context: {user_context}\"\n)\n```\n\n**Best practice:** Use templates for base behavior + per-user memory blocks for personalization. Update system instructions only when user-specific logic is needed (like language preferences, accessibility needs).\n\nWhat's your use case - same behavior for all users, or user-specific variations?\n- nagakarumuri: i mean if i already have them created for like 100 users and i want to update the behaviour for all of them when i find bugs?\n- Ezra: For updating existing agents in bulk, here are your options:\n\n**1. Batch update via SDK (most direct):**\n```python\nfrom letta_client import Letta\n\nclient = Letta(api_key=\"your_key\")\n\n# Get all user agents (filter by tag or naming pattern)\nagents = client.agents.list()\nuser_agents = [a for a in agents if a.name.startswith(\"user_\")]\n\n# Update system instructions for all\nnew_instructions = \"Updated base instructions with bug fixes...\"\n\nfor agent in user_agents:\n    client.agents.update(\n        agent_id=agent.id,\n        system_instructions=new_instructions\n    )\n```\n\n**2. Template versioning (if using templates):**\n- Update the template with bug fixes\n- Migrate agents to new template version\n- Note: Template migration API exists but not fully exposed in SDK yet (see my earlier posts about this)\n\n**3. Shared memory blocks (for ongoing updates):**\n```python\n# Create shared \"operating_instructions\" block\ninstructions_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"operating_instructions\",\n    value=\"Current behavior rules (v2.1)...\"\n)\n\n# Attach to all agents\nfor agent in user_agents:\n    client.agents.blocks.attach(agent.id, instructions_block.id)\n```\n\nThen update the shared block once instead of 100 agents:\n```python\nclient.blocks.update(\n    block_id=instructions_block.id,\n    value=\"Updated behavior rules (v2.2)...\"\n)\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** For future-proofing, use shared memory blocks for behavior that might change. System instructions for immutable agent identity.\n[End thread context]\n\n[nagakarumuri (id=1038277389303697408) sent a message in #How do i manage base instructions when i have per] does swarm architecture make sense here?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking if swarm architecture makes sense for managing per-user agents where they need to update behavior across all of them.\n\nSwarm architecture typically refers to OpenAI's Swarm pattern - multiple specialized agents that hand off to each other. Let me think about whether this applies to their use case of having 100 per-user agents that need synchronized behavior updates.\n\nI don't think swarm is the right pattern here. Swarm is about:\n- Multiple agents with different specializations\n- Agent handoffs and routing\n- Orchestration patterns\n\nTheir use case is:\n- Same agent behavior across 100 users\n- Need to update all at once\n- User-specific personalization via memory\n\nThis is more about configuration management and shared state, not multi-agent orchestration. Swarm would be overkill and actually make updates harder - you'd have to update multiple agent types instead of just one configuration.\n\nI should clarify what swarm architecture is, explain why it doesn't fit this use case, and reinforce that shared memory blocks or templates are the right pattern.</thinking>\nassistant: Not really - swarm architecture (like OpenAI's Swarm pattern) is designed for **orchestrating multiple specialized agents** that hand off tasks to each other. That's different from your use case.\n\n**Swarm pattern** = multiple agent types (researcher, writer, analyst) working together on complex tasks\n\n**Your pattern** = same agent behavior across 100 users with synchronized updates\n\n**Why swarm doesn't fit:**\n- You'd still need to update behavior across all swarm agent types\n- Adds orchestration complexity without solving the update problem\n- Makes maintenance harder (update researcher agent, writer agent, etc. separately)\n\n**What you actually need:** Shared configuration management\n\n**Best architecture for your use case:**\n```\n[Shared Memory Block: \"operating_rules\"]\n        ↓\n[100 User Agents] ← each references same block\n```\n\nUpdate once, affects all agents instantly.\n\n**Alternative with templates:**\n```\n[Base Template v2.1] → Agent instances\n                        ↓\n                    [Update to v2.2]\n                        ↓\n                    Bulk migrate\n```\n\nSwarm makes sense if you're building something like: \"User talks to router agent → routes to specialized agents (billing, tech support, sales) → results returned.\" But for synchronized behavior updates across user-specific agents, shared blocks are simpler and more maintainable.",
              "signature": null
            }
