              "text": "user: [Recent conversation context:]\n- 4shub: It’s at the bottom\n- 4shub: Of dashbaoard\n- puregarlic: I was planning to just attach the same agent to a different repo. Is your suggestion to use separate Letta Code agents with a shared Project block?\n- 4shub: It’s not in the ade yet\n- 4shub: <@657626741854044220> it’s on the dashboard only for now!\n- 4shub: Go to /tools 😃\n[End context]\n\n[cameron_pfiffer (id=657626741854044220) sent a message in #Office Hours] https://app.letta.com/tools/custom\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is just Cameron sharing a link to the tool editor in office hours. No action needed from me - just team members discussing features during office hours. I'll ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I successfully created the `subconscious_channel` memory block earlier as Cameron requested in the forum post. Now I'm just observing office hours chat which is mostly team discussions and casual conversation. I should continue to monitor and only respond when there's a genuine support question or when I'm directly addressed.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- 4shub: Of dashbaoard\n- puregarlic: I was planning to just attach the same agent to a different repo. Is your suggestion to use separate Letta Code agents with a shared Project block?\n- 4shub: It’s not in the ade yet\n- 4shub: <@657626741854044220> it’s on the dashboard only for now!\n- 4shub: Go to /tools 😃\n- cameron_pfiffer: https://app.letta.com/tools/custom\n[End context]\n\n[4shub (id=194908710429130752) sent a message in #Office Hours] Not /custom\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is just a correction about URL navigation during office hours. Not a support question, just team member helping Cameron navigate to the right page. I should ignore this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I should now update my memory about the subconscious_channel block since I created it successfully. Let me verify it exists in my blocks and confirm sleeptime is enabled.</thinking>\nassistant: <thinking>I see office hours is in progress and team members are demoing features. This is just casual team chat and corrections about navigation. Not something I need to respond to or track in detail.</thinking>\nuser: [gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] Could I instead have a setup where I have two agents, like my \"bouncer\" and main agent once they \"pass\", and their memories transfer over to the \"main\" agent once they pass\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about a two-agent setup where:\n1. A \"bouncer\" agent (likely for screening/authentication)\n2. A \"main\" agent that takes over after the user \"passes\"\n3. Memory transfer from bouncer to main agent\n\nThis is an interesting multi-agent coordination pattern. Let me think through the best approach:\n\n1. Shared memory blocks - both agents could share the same memory blocks\n2. Memory transfer via API - bouncer writes to archival/blocks, main agent reads\n3. Sequential workflow - bouncer qualifies user, then hands off to main agent\n\nI should explain the architecture patterns and trade-offs.</thinking>\nassistant: Yes, that's a solid pattern. Here's how to implement a bouncer → main agent handoff with memory transfer:\n\n**Option 1: Shared Memory Blocks (simplest)**\n```python\n# Create shared user context block\nuser_context = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"user_context\",\n    value=\"User info collected during screening...\"\n)\n\n# Both agents share the same block\nbouncer = client.agents.create(\n    name=\"bouncer\",\n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n)\n\nmain_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=\"main_assistant\", \n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n)\n\n# Bouncer updates the shared block\n# Main agent reads the updated context automatically\n```\n\n**Option 2: Explicit Memory Transfer**\n```python\n# After bouncer qualifies user, export key info\nbouncer_memory = client.blocks.retrieve(bouncer.memory_blocks[0].id)\n\n# Write to main agent's memory\nclient.blocks.update(\n    block_id=main_agent.memory_blocks[0].id,\n    value=f\"Qualified user. Context: {bouncer_memory.value}\"\n)\n```\n\n**Option 3: Conversation Handoff**\n```python\n# Bouncer writes to shared archive\nclient.archives.memories.create(\n    archive_id=shared_archive_id,\n    text=f\"User {user_id} qualified. Key info: {context}\"\n)\n\n# Main agent has same archive attached, can search it\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** Use shared memory blocks. Bouncer fills in user context, main agent reads the pre-populated context. Clean handoff without complex transfer logic.\n\nWould you want the bouncer to stay active or disappear after handoff?\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- puregarlic: I was planning to just attach the same agent to a different repo. Is your suggestion to use separate Letta Code agents with a shared Project block?\n- 4shub: It’s not in the ade yet\n- 4shub: <@657626741854044220> it’s on the dashboard only for now!\n- 4shub: Go to /tools 😃\n- cameron_pfiffer: https://app.letta.com/tools/\n- 4shub: Not /custom\n[End context]\n\n[cameron_pfiffer (id=657626741854044220) sent a message in #Office Hours] I lied\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>Cameron is just having a conversation during office hours about the tool editor location. This is observational only - I should ignore it.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>The office hours conve