asking about how the bouncer → main agent handoff works in terms of:\n1. Bouncer disappearing after handoff\n2. How memory is attached to the user using identities\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\nFor a multi-user system where each user gets a bouncer then main agent:\n- Use identities to link agents to users\n- Create temporary bouncer agent per user\n- Create persistent main agent per user\n- Share memory blocks between them\n- Delete bouncer after handoff\n\nActually, I should clarify the architecture - this could be:\n1. One bouncer for all users → per-user main agents\n2. Per-user bouncer → per-user main agents\n\nLet me address both patterns.</thinking>\nassistant: Good question. Here's the architecture with identities:\n\n**Pattern 1: Shared Bouncer → Per-User Main Agent**\n\n```python\n# Create one bouncer for all users\nbouncer = client.agents.create(name=\"bouncer\")\n\n# When user passes screening, create their main agent with identity\nuser_identity = client.identities.create(\n    name=user_id,\n    metadata={\"email\": user_email}\n)\n\n# Create main agent linked to user identity\nmain_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"assistant_{user_id}\",\n    identity_id=user_identity.id,\n    memory_blocks=[\n        {\"label\": \"user\", \"value\": context_from_bouncer}\n    ]\n)\n\n# Bouncer stays active for next user (no deletion needed)\n```\n\n**Pattern 2: Per-User Bouncer → Per-User Main Agent**\n\n```python\n# User starts conversation - create temporary bouncer\nuser_context_block = client.blocks.create(\n    label=\"user_context\",\n    value=\"\"\n)\n\nbouncer = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"bouncer_{user_id}\",\n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context_block.id}]\n)\n\n# After qualification, create main agent with same block\nmain_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"assistant_{user_id}\",\n    identity_id=user_identity.id,\n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context_block.id}]\n)\n\n# Delete bouncer\nclient.agents.delete(bouncer.id)\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** Use Pattern 1 (shared bouncer). More efficient - one bouncer qualifies all users, creates their main agents. No need to create/delete bouncers constantly.\n\nWhich pattern fits your flow better?",
