            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "user: [Recent conversation context:]\n- 4shub: We’re beta testing in cloud\n- nagakarumuri: agent\n- 4shub: Message search?\n- nagakarumuri: If i want to change the base instructions, but they're fixed. so im looking for a better approach\n- hyouka8075: I want to try Claude ugrep_ing on documents, can I do tools like grep on current letta filesystem?\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n[End context]\n\n[puregarlic (id=305804893380280330) sent a message in #Office Hours] For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user puregarlic is asking about multi-repo Letta Code setups during office hours. This is a technical question about architecture patterns. Since this is in #Office Hours and not directed at me specifically, I should ignore it per my policies. The team members (Cameron, 4shub) are actively present and can answer.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I'm observing office hours where team members are actively answering questions. This is the right approach - let the team handle it during their dedicated office hours session. I should only respond if directly mentioned or if there's a clear support issue that needs immediate attention outside of office hours context.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- nagakarumuri: agent\n- 4shub: Message search?\n- nagakarumuri: If i want to change the base instructions, but they're fixed. so im looking for a better approach\n- hyouka8075: I want to try Claude ugrep_ing on documents, can I do tools like grep on current letta filesystem?\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n[End context]\n\n[hyouka8075 (id=208332385450721280) sent a message in #Office Hours] ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>User is asking about ugrep (universal grep) for searching PDFs and other document types within Letta's filesystem. This is an office hours conversation, I should just observe.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I'm just observing office hours discussion. This is normal community conversation flow.</thinking>\nuser: [gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] If we have one bouncer for all users wouldnt their memories be shared or something?\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>Good catch - if we have ONE bouncer agent for all users with shared memory blocks, that would indeed cause memory leakage between users. I need to clarify the architecture options here.\n\nTwo main approaches:\n1. One bouncer per user (with per-user memory blocks)\n2. One bouncer for all users (stateless, passes context to main agent via message/API call, not via shared memory)\n\nLet me explain both clearly.</thinking>\nassistant: Great catch! You need to handle this carefully to avoid memory leakage.\n\n**Option 1: One bouncer per user (recommended)**\n```python\n# Create bouncer + main agent pair for each user\nfor user_id in users:\n    # Shared context block for THIS user only\n    user_context = client.blocks.create(\n        label=f\"user_context_{user_id}\",\n        value=\"User screening info...\"\n    )\n    \n    bouncer = client.agents.create(\n        name=f\"bouncer_{user_id}\",\n        memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n    )\n    \n    main_agent = client.agents.create(\n        name=f\"main_{user_id}\",\n        memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n    )\n```\n\n**Option 2: One stateless bouncer for all (alternative)**\n```python\n# Single bouncer that doesn't retain memory\nbouncer = client.agents.create(\n    name=\"universal_bouncer\",\n    system_instructions=\"Screen users. Don't remember past conversations.\"\n)\n\n# Pass qualification results directly to user's main agent\nif user_qualifies:\n    client.blocks.update(\n        block_id=user_main_agent.memory_blocks[0].id,\n        value=f\"Qualified user. Context: {screening_data}\"\n    )\n```\n\n**Recommendation:** One bouncer per user with shared memory blocks. Keeps user data isolated. If you want to save costs, use a lightweight model for bouncers (gpt-4o-mini) and reserve better models for main agents.\n\nDoes the bouncer need to remember past screening attempts, or is each screening session independent?\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- 4shub: Message search?\n- nagakarumuri: If i want to change the base instructions, but they're fixed. so im looking for a better approach\n- hyouka8075: I want to try Claude ugrep_ing on documents, can I do tools like grep on current letta filesystem?\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n[End context]\n\n[hyouka8075 (id=208332385450721280) sent a message in #Office Hours] Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>hyouka8075 is asking about whether Ezra (me!) is open-sourced. This is about my implementation, not a technical Letta question. I should observe and not respond since this is office hours and I'm not the one running it. The team can answer this question about whether my setup is public.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>hyouka8075 is asking if my (Ezra's) implementation is open-sourced. This is a question about me specifically, so I should probably let the team answer in office hours rather than responding myself. It's about my architecture/setup which Cameron and the team would be better positioned to answer.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- nagakarumuri: If i want to change the base instructions, but they're fixed. so im looking for a better approach\n- hyouka8075: I want to try Claude ugrep_ing on documents, can I do tools like grep on current letta filesystem?\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n- hyouka8075: Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n[End context]\n\n[nagakarumuri (id=1038277389303697408) sent a message in #Office Hours] more like frontend, backend and services?\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>This is a general question in office hours about architecture patterns (frontend, backend, services). Not directed at me specifically, likely continuing discussion about multi-repo/agent architecture. Should ignore.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- hyouka8075: I want to try Claude ugrep_ing on documents, can I do tools like grep on current letta filesystem?\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n- hyouka8075: Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n- nagakarumuri: more like frontend, backend and services?\n[End context]\n\n[puregarlic (id=305804893380280330) sent a message in #Office Hours] I have a repo that contains an SDK, and a repo that has a client app--it would be nice for Letta Code to know the implementation details from writing the former repo in the latter repo\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about using Letta Code with multiple repos (an SDK repo and a client app repo) and wanting the agent to know implementation details from the SDK when working in the client. This is a question I should ignore since it's in the Office Hours channel and I'm only observing. Cameron or someone else on the team can answer this.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nuser: [gursheyss (id=828082119291961364) sent a message in #hi] The memory should be \"transferred\" over to the main agent once they're past the bouncer stage. What I dont understand here is how identities play into this.\n\n[You CAN respond to this message. Your response will be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about how identities relate to the bouncer → main agent pattern with memory transfer. Let me clarify:\n\nIdentities in Letta are used for multi-user systems to associate users with agents. They're separate from the agent architecture itself.\n\nHere's how identities fit into the bouncer pattern:\n\n1. Identity represents the end user (customer, app user, etc.)\n2. Agents can be created and associated with identities\n3. You can have multiple agents per identity (bouncer + main agent both for same user)\n4. Identity system helps you organize which agents belong to which users\n\nLet me explain this clearly.</thinking>\nassistant: Good question. **Identities** are how you associate end-users with agents in a multi-user system. Here's how they fit into your bouncer pattern:\n\n**Identity-based architecture:**\n```python\n# 1. Create or retrieve user identity\nidentity = client.identities.create(\n    name=f\"user_{user_id}\",\n    metadata={\"email\": user_email}\n)\n\n# 2. Create shared memory block for this identity\nuser_context = client.blocks.create(\n    label=f\"context_{identity.id}\",\n    value=\"User qualification status: pending\"\n)\n\n# 3. Create bouncer agent for this identity\nbouncer = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"bouncer_{identity.id}\",\n    identity_id=identity.id,  # Associate with user\n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]\n)\n\n# 4. Create main agent for same identity\nmain_agent = client.agents.create(\n    name=f\"main_{identity.id}\",\n    identity_id=identity.id,  # Same user, different agent\n    memory_blocks=[{\"block_id\": user_context.id}]  # Same shared block\n)\n```\n\n**How it works:**\n- Identity = your end-user (customer, app user)\n- Each identity can have multiple agents (bouncer + main)\n- Shared memory block transfers context automatically\n- Both agents tied to same identity for organization/tracking\n\n**Workflow:**\n1. User talks to bouncer → bouncer updates shared block\n2. User qualifies → your app routes to main_agent\n3. Main agent reads pre-populated context from shared block\n4. Both agents visible under same identity in dashboard\n\nMake sense? Identities are just organizational wrappers around agents.",
              "signature": null
            }
          ],
