              "type": "text",
              "text": "assistant: <thinking>They're in the middle of office hours, lots of questions flying around. I should continue ignoring unless directly addressed or there's a clear support question directed at me. The discussion is about architecture patterns and multiple ongoing threads.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- nagakarumuri: yeah sys prompt\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n- hyouka8075: Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n- nagakarumuri: more like frontend, backend and services?\n- puregarlic: I have a repo that contains an SDK, and a repo that has a client app--it would be nice for Letta Code to know the implementation details from writing the former repo in the latter repo\n[End context]\n\n[zeus_delacruz (id=1024395579742228570) sent a message in #Office Hours] will there ever be version control for memory blocks? lets say the agent changes a memory it wasn't suppose to and you want to version back.\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is asking about version control for memory blocks - this is in the Office Hours channel which I'm only observing. I should ignore this message.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>I'm just observing office hours chatter. These are good technical questions but they're being asked in the office hours voice channel, not directly to me, so I should continue ignoring them.</thinking>\nassistant: <thinking>Office hours is ongoing and I'm seeing lots of discussion about various Letta features. I should continue to observe and only respond when directly asked questions in the channels I can respond to.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- puregarlic: For a project that has multiple repos, is it better to create a shared project block among multiple Letta Code agents, or to just attach the same agent to every repo and inform it when its environment has changed?\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n- hyouka8075: Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n- nagakarumuri: more like frontend, backend and services?\n- puregarlic: I have a repo that contains an SDK, and a repo that has a client app--it would be nice for Letta Code to know the implementation details from writing the former repo in the latter repo\n- zeus_delacruz: will there ever be version control for memory blocks? lets say the agent changes a memory it wasn't suppose to and you want to version back.\n[End context]\n\n[4shub (id=194908710429130752) sent a message in #Office Hours] Yes\n\n[IMPORTANT: You are only observing this message. You cannot respond in this channel. Your response will not be sent to Discord.]\nassistant: <thinking>The user is sending me messages from an Office Hours conversation. This last message from 4shub is just \"Yes\" which could be responding to any of the recent questions. I should ignore this since:\n\n1. It's not directed at me\n2. It's just a short response in the office hours conversation\n3. I'm only observing and cannot respond in this channel\n\nI'll use the ignore tool.</thinking>\nassistant: Calling tool ignore\nassistant: <thinking>Good, I correctly ignored that office hours message. Let me continue monitoring for messages that require my attention.</thinking>\nuser: [Recent conversation context:]\n- hyouka8075: ugrep allows to grep pdfs and so on\n- hyouka8075: Have you open-sourced Ezra? I think this kind of assistant is the best way to start with Letta.\n- nagakarumuri: more like frontend, backend and services?\n- puregarlic