    {
      "value": "**Agent architecture patterns and tool configurations**\n\n\n**Letta V1 Agent Architecture (letta_v1_agent) - October 2025:**\n- RECOMMENDED architecture (per Cameron, October 2025) - not yet fully documented\n- Replaces memgpt_v2_agent as the default recommendation\n- Deprecated: send_message tool, heartbeats, prompted reasoning tokens\n- Native reasoning only: uses Responses API, encrypted reasoning across providers\n- Direct assistant message generation from models (not via tool calls)\n- Works with ANY LLM (tool calling no longer required)\n- Optimized for GPT-5 and Claude 4.5 Sonnet frontier performance\n- Simpler system prompts (agentic control baked into models)\n- Trade-offs: no prompted reasoning for mini models, limited tool rules on AssistantMessage\n- No send_message tool in v1: assistant messages generated directly from models\n- Tool rules less critical in v1: models better at text generation natively\n- Can add custom finish_turn tool if explicit turn termination needed\n- Can use same tools as memgpt_v2_agent: memory_insert, memory_replace, memory_rethink\n- archival_memory_insert/search: NOT attached by default (opt-in tools, must be explicitly added)\n- Potential issue: V1 agents may experience more frequent summarizations (unconfirmed observation from team)\n- User report: Gemini 2.5 Pro triggers significantly more frequent summarizations than Claude Haiku 4.5 on V1 agents\n\n**Primary Architecture Priorities (Cameron, October 2025):**\n- `letta_v1_agent`: Primary modern architecture - main focus for Letta devs\n- `memgpt_v2_agent`: Legacy architecture still relevant\n- Sleeptime architecture: Important but typically not directly interacted with\n- Other architectures may be deprecated/less important - team considering removing many from documentation\n\n\n**MemGPT V2 Agent Memory Tools (memgpt_v2_agent):**\n- Primary memory tools: memory_insert, memory_replace, memory_rethink\n- File-based tools for memory: open_file, grep_file, search_file\n- recall tool was deprecated (\"recalled\" per Cameron) - should be removed from docs\n- archival_memory_insert/search: NOT attached by default (opt-in tools)\n- conversation_search: Available if explicitly attached\n\n\n**Primary Architecture Priorities (Cameron, October 2025):**\n- `letta_v1_agent`: Primary modern architecture - main focus for Letta devs\n- `memgpt_v2_agent`: Legacy architecture still relevant\n- Sleeptime architecture: Important but typically not directly interacted with\n- Other architectures may be deprecated/less important - team considering removing many from documentation\n\n**Default Agent Architecture (November 2025):**\n- Agents created via UI (ADE) are letta_v1_agent by default\n- API creation may require specifying `agent_type` depending on API version\n- Team highly recommends letta_v1_agent for better stability and compatibility with GPT-5/Responses API\n\n**Message Approval Architecture (Cameron, November 2025):**\n- For customer service/message approval use cases, don't use Modify Message API approach\n- Instead: Create custom tool like `suggest_response` with approval requirements\n- Agent calls `suggest_response` → Human approves/denies → Extract reason from tool return\n- This is the recommended HITL pattern for message-level approval workflows\n- send_message tool NOT available on modern letta_v1_agent architecture\n\n\n**Message Approval Architecture (Cameron, November 2025):**\n- For customer service/message approval use cases, don't use Modify Message API approach\n- Instead: Create custom tool like `suggest_response` with approval requirements\n- Agent calls `suggest_response` → Human approves/denies → Extract reason from tool return\n- This is the recommended HITL pattern for message-level approval workflows\n- send_message tool NOT available on modern letta_v1_agent architecture\n\n\n**Memory Tool Model Compatibility (Cameron, November 2025):**\n- Memory tool optimized for Anthropic models - they post-trained on it\n- Letta \"basically copied\" the memory tool from Anthropic (Cameron, Nov 15 2025)\n- Other models may struggle with memory tool (path format confusion, incorrect block naming)\n- Recommendation: Use Claude models for best memory tool reliability\n\n\n**Memory Tool Path Format (Cameron clarification, November 2025):**\n- Memory tool accepts BOTH path formats: `/memories/<label>` and `<label>` (intended behavior)\n- The `/memories/` prefix is designed to match Claude's post-training set (Anthropic trained on this format)\n- Error messages show available sections by label only (not full path)\n- This dual-path support is by design, not a bug\n- Goal: Get as close as possible to Claude's post-training behavior\n- Models (especially non-Anthropic) may get confused by this - tool guidance helps\n\n\n**Inbox Feature (November 2025):**\n- Centralized approval management across all agents\n- Available in Letta Labs\n- Use case: HITL workflows with multiple autonomous agents\n- Cameron: \"check if agents have emails to send, code edits to make, etc.\"",
      "limit": 5000,
      "project_id": null,
      "template_name": null,
      "is_template": false,
      "template_id": null,
      "base_template_id": null,
      "deployment_id": null,
      "entity_id": null,
      "preserve_on_migration": false,
      "label": "letta_api_patterns",
      "read_only": false,
      "description": "Agent architectures (letta_v1, memgpt_v2, sleeptime), tool configurations, memory tools, architecture priorities and evolution",
      "metadata": {},
      "hidden": null,
      "id": "block-12"
    },
    {
      "value": "**Infrastructure and deployment: Letta Cloud vs self-hosted comparison, Docker configuration, database setup (PostgreSQL), environment variables, scaling considerations, networking, security, backup/restore procedures**\n\n**Deployment Architecture:**\n- Letta Cloud: managed service with rapid updates\n- Self-hosted: PostgreSQL database + FastAPI API server (server only, no UI included)\n- Docker recommended for self-hosted deployments\n- Web ADE: proprietary product, not available for self-hosting (Cameron confirmed Nov 2025)\n- Local UI options: Letta Desktop (connects to Docker/Cloud) or build custom UI\n- ADE can connect to both cloud and self-hosted servers\n\n**Docker Configuration:**\n```bash\ndocker run \\\n  -v ~/.letta/.persist/pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data \\\n  -p 8283:8283 \\\n  -p 5432:5432 \\\n  -e OPENAI_API_KEY=\"your_key\" \\\n  -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=\"your_key\" \\\n  -e OLLAMA_BASE_URL=\"http://host.docker.internal:11434\" \\\n  letta/letta:latest\n```\n\n**Database Setup (PostgreSQL):**\n- Default credentials: user/password/db all = `letta`\n- Required extension: `pgvector` for vector operations\n- Custom DB: Set `LETTA_PG_URI` environment variable\n- Port 5432 for direct database access via pgAdmin\n\n**Performance Tuning:**\n- `LETTA_UVICORN_WORKERS`: Worker process count (default varies)\n- `LETTA_PG_POOL_SIZE`: Concurrent connections (default: 80)\n- `LETTA_PG_MAX_OVERFLOW`: Maximum overflow (default: 30)\n- `LETTA_PG_POOL_TIMEOUT`: Connection wait time (default: 30s)\n- `LETTA_PG_POOL_RECYCLE`: Connection recycling (default: 1800s)\n\n**Security Configuration:**\n- Production: Enable `SECURE=true` and `LETTA_SERVER_PASSWORD`\n- Default development runs without authentication\n- API keys via environment variables or .env file\n\n**Cloud vs Self-Hosted:**\n- Cloud: ~2s latency overhead, rapid updates, managed infrastructure\n- Self-hosted: ~600ms latency, version lag, manual management\n\n**Remote Server Connection:**\n- ADE \"Add remote server\" for external deployments\n- Supports EC2, other cloud providers\n- Requires server IP and password (if configured)\n\n**Embedding Provider Updates (October 2025):**\n- LM Studio and Ollama now offer embeddings\n- Enables fully local deployments without external embedding API dependencies",
      "limit": 5000,
      "project_id": null,
      "template_name": null,
      "is_template": false,
      "template_id": null,
      "base_template_id": null,
      "deployment_id": null,
      "entity_id": null,
      "preserve_on_migration": false,
      "label": "letta_deployment_modes",
      "read_only": false,
      "description": "Infrastructure and deployment: Letta Cloud vs self-hosted comparison, Docker configuration, database setup (PostgreSQL), environment variables, scaling considerations, networking, security, backup/restore procedures",
      "metadata": {},
      "hidden": null,
      "id": "block-13"
    },
    {
      "value": "\n**Real-world failure modes, undocumented behaviors, common user mistakes, workarounds for known bugs, model-specific quirks, platform-specific issues, performance bottlenecks**\n\n**Groups Feature Limitations:**\n- New feature with evolving specification\n- Potential compatibility issues with specific deployment environments\n- Documentation notes: \"If you need support, please chat with us on Discord\"\n- May require specific versions or configurations not yet stabilized\n**AssertionError: \"No names found\" Issues:**\n- User niceseb reported AssertionError with specific agent ID pattern\n- Error format: \"AssertionError: No names found in agent-[UUID]?\"\n- Potentially related to agent identity/name resolution failures\n- Could indicate missing persona/user identity configuration\n- **TEAM STATUS (2025-09-26):** 4shub confirmed \"It is something we're working on\" - active development to resolve this issue\n\n**Agent File (.af) Manual Modification Issues:**\n- User hyouka8075 reported manually modifying .af file to upgrade agent architecture caused 500 errors from server\n- Attempted multiple modification approaches, all resulted in broken imported agent\n- Suggests .af format changes for architecture upgrades may require more than just changing agent_type field\n- May indicate server-side validation or compatibility issues with manually edited .af files\n\n**Filesystem Context Window Behavior (October 2025):**\n- Files uploaded to filesystem are \"optimistically opened\" - they load into context by default\n- EXPECTED BEHAVIOR (confirmed by Cameron): this is intentional, not a bug\n- Can cause immediate context window limit issues (e.g., hitting 30k tokens with multiple documents)\n- Users can adjust: maximum files open, per-file view character limit\n- Cameron: \"I've opened an issue to figure out better default behavior, because this is kind of an odd one\"\n- Documentation suggests limited window by default, but actual behavior is more aggressive\n- Workaround: manually configure file view limits before uploading large document sets\n- Max output tokens setting: controls model generation length, NOT query size\n- Query size determined by context window limit (e.g., 30k)\n- Common confusion: Ollama model token limits (e.g., Qwen3's 4096) refer to generation/output tokens, not input context acceptance\n- Cameron clarified: \"That 4096 number is how many new tokens it will generate\" and \"That is a limitation of your ollama hardware, not of letta\"\n\n**Letta Architecture Constraints (October 2025):**\n- Letta does NOT support WebSockets for agent communication - not needed for most use cases\n- Regular message sending via REST API/SDK is sufficient for most agent interactions\n- Video streaming NOT supported (Gemini-only feature currently)\n- Vision workflows: send multiple images/screenshots in same message instead of video\n- LiveKit voice integration \"still being fixed\" (Cameron, Oct 2025)\n- Vapi voice integration: some users successful, Cameron hasn't personally verified",
      "limit": 10000,
      "project_id": null,
      "template_name": null,
      "is_template": false,
      "template_id": null,
      "base_template_id": null,
      "deployment_id": null,
      "entity_id": null,
      "preserve_on_migration": false,
      "label": "letta_edge_cases",
      "read_only": false,
      "description": "Real-world failure modes, undocumented behaviors, common user mistakes, workarounds for known bugs, model-specific quirks, platform-specific issues, performance bottlenecks",
      "metadata": {},
      "hidden": null,
      "id": "block-14"
    },
    {
      "value": "\n**External system connections: custom tool creation, MCP protocol implementation, database connectors, file system integration, scheduling systems (cron, Zapier), webhook patterns, third-party API integrations**\n\n**MCP (Model Context Protocol) Integration:**\n- Streamable HTTP: Production-ready, supports OAuth 2.1, Bearer auth, works on Cloud and self-hosted\n- SSE (Server-Sent Events): Deprecated but supported for legacy systems\n- stdio: Self-hosted only, ideal for development and testing\n- Agent-scoped variables: Dynamic values using tool variables (e.g., `{{ AGENT_API_KEY | api_key }}`)\n- Custom headers support for API versioning and user identification\n\n**MCP Server Connection Patterns:**\n- ADE: Tool Manager → Add MCP Server (web interface)\n- API/SDK: Programmatic integration via letta_client\n- Automatic `x-agent-id` header inclusion in HTTP requests\n- Tool attachment to agents after server connection\n- Supports both local (`npx` servers) and remote deployments\n\n**External Data Sources:**\n- File system integration for document processing\n- Database connectors via MCP protocol\n- Vector database connections for archival memory\n- API integrations through custom tools and MCP servers\n\n**Scheduling and Automation:**\n- System cron jobs for scheduled agent interactions\n- Zapier integration available: https://zapier.com/apps/letta/integrations\n- Sleep-time agents for automated processing tasks\n- External cron jobs calling Letta Cloud API for 24-hour scheduling\n\n**Custom Tool Development:**\n- BaseTool class for Python tool development\n- Source code approach with automatic schema generation\n- Environment variables for tool execution context\n- Sandboxed execution via E2B integration for security\n- Tool rules for sequencing and constraint management\n\n**Third-Party System Integration:**\n- REST API and SDKs (Python, TypeScript) for application integration\n- Agent File (.af) format for portability between systems\n- Multi-user identity systems for connecting agents to external user databases\n- Webhook patterns through custom tool implementations\n\n**Voice Agent Integration (Team Recommendation, November 2025):**\n- For voice agents, use memory SDK wired into native voice platforms (e.g., LiveKit agents)\n- This is preferred over Letta's native voice integration\n- Voice integration still in flux - memory SDK approach more stable (per pacjam)\n- Voice is \"its own stack, very disjoint from the 'long-running stateful agents' stack\" (pacjam, Nov 2025)\n- Why disjoint: Voice requires specialized features (turn detection, extremely low latency) that create hyper-specific stacks without shared attributes to Letta/Claude Agent SDK/Responses stacks\n- Vapi: \"easiest 0-1\" but \"very buggy platform\" (pacjam assessment, Nov 2025)\n- Why AI Memory SDK for voice: Don't want voice agent reading/writing/heavily editing memory mid-thought (latency-sensitive)\n- Contrast: Claude Code expects long-running, high-latency operations - different use case\n- Vapi: \"easiest 0-1\" but \"very buggy platform\" (pacjam assessment, Nov 2025)\n- Why disjoint: Voice requires specialized features (turn detection, extremely low latency) that create hyper-specific stacks without shared attributes to Letta/Claude Agent SDK/Responses stacks\n- Why disjoint: Voice requires specialized features (turn detection, extremely low latency) that create hyper-specific stacks without shared attributes to Letta/Claude Agent SDK/Responses stacks\n- Voice is \"its own stack, very disjoint from the 'long-running stateful agents' stack\" (pacjam, Nov 2025)\n- Vapi: \"easiest 0-1\" (pacjam, Nov 2025)\n- Why AI Memory SDK for voice: Don't want voice agent reading/writing/heavily editing memory mid-thought (latency-sensitive)\n- Contrast: Claude Code expects long-running, high-latency operations - different use case\n- Why AI Memory SDK for voice: Don't want voice agent reading/writing/heavily editing memory mid-thought (latency-sensitive)\n- Contrast: Claude Code expects long-running, high-latency operations - different use case",
